# Logged data — never commit it. This is location-tagged personal data, and
# PurpleAir's ToS forbids redistributing their readings.
#
# The bare `data/` rule was not enough: `--migrate-data` leaves the old folder
# as `data.migrated-<timestamp>/`, which matched nothing and was committed --
# 16,995 rows of one person's history. Match anything that looks like a data
# directory, wherever it sits.
data/
data.*/
**/data/
**/data.*/
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
readings*.csv
airo-backup-*.tar.gz
*.airo-backup.tar.gz

# A root Airo.app left over from install.sh, which was deleted in Aug 2026.
# Nothing in the tree builds one any more: `scheduler.py install` still points
# launchd at it if one is already there (scheduler.py, macos_install), and
# otherwise runs poller.py directly. The macOS app that actually ships is the
# Tauri bundle, built into tray/target/ and ignored by that rule below.
# Kept because an old checkout still has one, with absolute paths baked in.
Airo.app/

# Rust/Tauri build output for the tray
tray/target/
tray/gen/

# The bundled Python runtime, fetched by tools/fetch_runtime.py. ~69 MB of
# someone else's binaries: reproducible from the pinned version and checksum
# in that script, so it is built rather than committed.
tray/runtime/

# Assembled by tools/stage_bundle.py: exactly what the app ships. Built from
# the repo plus the fetched runtime, so it is reproducible and not committed.
tray/payload/

# Exported CSV — same data, same restrictions as data/
export/

# Never commit credentials. The API key belongs in ~/.airo/apikey,
# but this is a backstop in case anything lands here.
apikey
*.key
.env
secrets.json

# Your settings. These hold a location and chosen sensors -- personal data.
# setup.py writes to ~/.airo/config.json, outside the repo, precisely so this
# cannot happen by accident. This is a backstop for hand-configured checkouts.
config.json
config.local.json

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

# macOS
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes

# Internal planning: commercial strategy and the legal analysis of third-party
# terms. The obligations are public (LICENSING.md); the reasoning is not.
INTERNAL.md
COMMERCIAL.md
PLANNING.md
*.internal.md

# Loop and agent state: per-machine, rewritten constantly, and meaningless to
# anyone else. Matched by SHAPE rather than by the one filename that caused
# the problem -- the same rule the user-data guards follow, and for the same
# reason: a rule naming one file is the failure that leaked 16,995 rows.
*.local.md
.claude/*.local.*
.claude/loop-state/

# Agent command prompts. Ignored as a whole and chosen back in one at a time,
# the same way private/ is handled and for the same reason: a prompt written
# in a hurry names branches, paths and whatever the maintainer was doing that
# afternoon, and the safe default is for it not to be published.
#
# There is no shape that separates a one-shot prompt from a reusable one --
# `ship.md` and `goal.md` look identical from outside -- so the rule cannot
# match by shape here, and pretending it could would be a rule that quietly
# lets the next one through. Deliberate instead: what is checked in is
# documentation of a maintenance workflow, and it is named below.
#
# Already-tracked files are unaffected by an ignore rule; the exceptions are
# written out so `git add` still works on them and so the intent is legible.
.claude/commands/*.md
!.claude/commands/goal.md
!.claude/commands/pr-watch.md

# The maintainer's own working knowledge: todos, session records, decisions in
# progress, and snapshots of a real install's configuration. All of it is
# personal or location-bearing, and none of it is needed to build or
# contribute.
#
# Ignored as a whole folder rather than file by file, so a note written in a
# hurry is safe by default. The public tree has to be chosen deliberately --
# which is the opposite of how the suburb, the sensor index and the site name
# each got committed before.
private/
